{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>The camel, etc., and the rock-badger, etc., and the hare, etc., and the swine, etc.</b> The Torah permitted no domesticated or wild animal except by these two signs, namely split hooves and chewing the cud. And the reason it did not mention the horse and the donkey is that they possess not a single one of these signs; therefore it needed to specify these four, in each of which there is one sign of purity and they are nevertheless impure, since they do not have both signs of fitness but only one alone, and the Torah permitted only with two. And when Scripture mentioned the swine, it included the dog as well, for it is like it in that it does not chew the cud but its hooves are split; and so the Prophet likened them one to the other, as it says (Isaiah 66:3): ״זובח השה עורף כלב מעלה מנחה דם חזיר״. And it mentioned three animals in masculine language, and it is known that the females are included in the prohibition, since the female is included in the male. And similarly with the hare — had it written הארנב (the male hare), the female would have been included in it as with the rest; but Scripture needed to mention the hare in feminine language, to hint that the species of the hare are androgynous, possessing both male and female organs, and they both beget and give birth." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> It mentions these four animals here in four verses, each animal in a verse by itself, to allude to the four kingdoms that existed at different times, each and every one a separate exile. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: את הגמל — this is the kingdom of Babylon, as it says (Psalms 137:8): ״בת בבל השדודה אשרי שישלם לך את גמולך שגמלת לנו״. את השפן — this is Greece, which annulled the Torah from the mouth of the prophets, as it says (Amos 8:11): ״הנה ימים באים נאם ה' אלהים והשלחתי רעב בארץ לא רעב ללחם ולא צמא למים כי אם לשמוע את דברי ה'״, and it is written (ibid.): ״ונעו מים עד ים״. ואת הארנבת — this is Media, which made Israel a ḥefker (ownerless) for destruction, slaughter, and annihilation; and the name of the wife of Ptolemy was Arnevet. ואת החזיר — this is the kingdom of the fourth exile, as it says (Psalms 80:14): ״יכרסמנה חזיר מיער״. But in Mishneh Torah, in the section Re'eh, it mentioned three of them in one verse, as it is written (Deuteronomy 14:7): ״את הגמל ואת הארנבת ואת השפן כי מעלה גרה המה״ etc., and it mentioned the fourth in a verse by itself, as it is written: ״ואת החזיר כי מפריס פרסה הוא ולא גרה״ — and this was to allude that the three exiles, even though their times were separate, were as though they were joined together, for their duration was brief and short; but the fourth exile it mentioned by itself both in this parashah and in Mishneh Torah, to indicate that it is a long exile by itself and is weighed against all of them. And corresponding to these four kingdoms, King David, peace be upon him, mentioned four expressions of thanksgiving in the psalm called the Great Hallel. He first mentioned (Psalms 136:1): ״הודו לה' כי טוב כי לעולם חסדו״, for the kingdom of Babylon; and he mentioned a second time ״הודו לאלהי האלהים״, for the kingdom of Greece; and he mentioned a third time ״הודו לאדני האדונים״, for the kingdom of Media. And he mentioned three consecutive expressions of הודו corresponding to the three animals that Moses mentioned together; and he mentioned in the final verse of the psalm ״הודו לאל השמים״, corresponding to the kingdom of Edom. And because he saw in the holy spirit salvation and deliverance for Israel from all of them, therefore he gave thanksgiving corresponding to all of them; and he mentioned 'the heavens' in this thanksgiving because in this redemption the heavens will rejoice and the earth will exult." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> [Tanhuma]: Why was this kingdom likened to a swine? Because the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to restore upon them the attribute of judgment. And there are versions in which it is written that He is destined to restore the crown to its former state. And the meaning is that the two Temples were built by Israel: the First Temple was built by Solomon, who is of the seed of Judah; the Second Temple was built by Zerubbabel, who was also of the seed of Judah, as it says (Zechariah 4:9): ״ידי זרובבל יסדו״ etc., and it was accomplished through Cyrus, who granted permission for this. But the Third Temple, this nation is destined to build it; and this is what they said: 'He is destined to restore the crown to its former state,' because it was they who destroyed it." "<b>And there are</b> versions that read: 'The Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to restore it to us.' And the masses understand that what will be restored will be pure for Israel. But the explanation of the matter concerns its power — namely that which afflicts Israel — and in the future it will turn, along with all the other forces, to aid and support Israel, for peace will abound in the world, as it says (Isaiah 11:9): ״וגר זאב עם כבש״ etc., and it is written (ibid.): ״לא ירעו ולא ישחיתו בכל הר קדשי״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:24:56.404672736Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}